framework ArchitectureDecisionRecord:
  requires problem and decision
  requires premise and evidence
  warns pending

domain ADR0024:
  description "Detach asset authoring execution from read traffic"

problem P1:
  |
    The six authenticated draft GET handlers call wakeCharacterAuthoringJobs
    before reading draft state. That helper claims the oldest pending job across
    all three asset families and starts provider work in the current Cloud Run
    process. The queue-wide claim is valid worker behavior, but invoking it from
    a read makes polling an execution trigger. A pending job also has no durable
    wake after request loss, process restart, or scale-to-zero. These behaviors
    contradict ADR-0014 rules 1, 2, and 7.

premise DRIVER_READ_PURITY:
  |
    Draft GET routes are projections only. They may authenticate, authorize, and
    read, but must not claim work, change attempt state, call a provider, or
    become required for progress.

premise DRIVER_DURABILITY:
  |
    An accepted create, revise, or upgrade command must persist both the work and
    its wake intent atomically. Progress after the command response cannot rely
    on an in-process promise, later user polling, or a particular process
    surviving.

premise DRIVER_OWNERSHIP:
  |
    The worker queue and provider-capacity admission are global to the deployed
    environment, not partitioned by user. Once a job is selected, every attempt,
    candidate, asset, generation, and notification operation must remain bound
    to that job's persisted owner and entity under a fenced lease. Public or
    cross-user visibility grants no authoring, activation, deletion, or pointer-
    move authority.

premise DRIVER_EXISTING_CONTRACT:
  |
    ADR-0014 already requires an authoring-owned outbox and fence and forbids
    sharing narration tables. ADR-0006 supplies the accepted durable Cloud Tasks
    wake pattern. This ADR completes those requirements for all three authoring
    families; it does not replace owner acceptance or asset revision authority.

premise OPTIONS:
  |
    Considered options are: delete GET wake calls and retain mutation-time
    fire-and-forget; add a process-local startup drain; dispatch a durable generic
    wake that lets the environment-global worker claim the next job; or persist
    one authoring outbox delivery per job and address that job explicitly inside
    the same environment-global queue. Fire-and-forget and startup-only draining
    strand work under scale-to-zero or process loss. Generic wake is compatible
    with the ownership model but makes wake-to-job completion and lost-delivery
    reconciliation indirect. Exact delivery is proposed for traceable liveness
    and idempotent recovery, not to create a per-user queue or user authority.

evidence E_ROUTES:
  |
    backend/src/routes.ts calls wakeCharacterAuthoringJobs from character,
    battlefield, and narration-style latest/by-id GET handlers as well as from
    mutation handlers.

evidence E_WORKER:
  |
    backend/src/services/character-authoring-jobs.ts implements wake as an
    unawaited processNextCharacterAuthoringJob call. That processor invokes
    claimNextFamilyAuthoringJob without an attempt identity.

evidence E_CLAIM:
  |
    backend/src/repositories/family-authoring-jobs.ts unions pending jobs from
    all three family tables, orders them globally by created_at, and claims the
    first capacity-eligible row. This is consistent with an environment-global
    worker queue. Each selected row carries owner_user_id and asset_id into the
    family runner; ownership safety depends on preserving those values through
    every repository operation.

evidence E_OWNER_BOUNDARY:
  |
    On 2026-09-08 the product owner clarified that the Worker Queue is global to
    the environment, while one worker target execution must not cross its user
    boundary. Entities may be visible or usable across users, but authoring and
    other mutations remain restricted to the entity owner unless a separate
    explicit operator or system authority applies.

evidence OWNER_ACCEPTANCE:
  |
    On 2026-09-08, after the related-ADR consistency review corrected the user-
    boundary defect in this record, the product owner explicitly approved this
    revised ADR-0024.

evidence E_DURABLE_PATTERN:
  |
    backend/src/services/narration-task-dispatch.ts and
    backend/src/services/narration-worker.ts implement deterministic Cloud Tasks
    delivery from a durable narration outbox with delivery generations, OIDC
    authorization, stale recovery, and fenced exact work.

decision D1 based_on P1, DRIVER_READ_PURITY, E_ROUTES:
  |
    Propose removing every authoring wake from the six draft GET handlers and
    regression-locking them as read-only. Repeated latest and by-id reads must
    produce zero authoring job claims, attempt transitions, outbox mutations,
    notifications, and provider calls.

decision D2 based_on DRIVER_DURABILITY, DRIVER_EXISTING_CONTRACT, OPTIONS, E_DURABLE_PATTERN:
  |
    Propose an authoring-owned outbox persisted in the same transaction that
    inserts or reopens a character, battlefield, or narration-style job. Each
    row records family, attempt ID, delivery generation, pending or dispatched
    state, delivery attempts, creation time, and dispatch time. It never shares
    battle_narration_outbox.

decision D3 based_on DRIVER_OWNERSHIP, OPTIONS, E_WORKER, E_CLAIM, E_OWNER_BOUNDARY, D2:
  |
    Propose an OIDC-authenticated internal authoring task endpoint whose payload
    identifies exactly one outbox ID, family, attempt ID, and generation inside
    the environment-global queue. The handler resolves the owner and entity from
    persisted job state rather than accepting them from the delivery, then
    claims that job with a unique worker identity and fenced lease. Exact
    addressing supplies wake-to-job liveness and idempotency evidence; it does
    not partition scheduling or grant the triggering request mutation authority.
    Capacity saturation returns a retryable response for that delivery.

decision D4 based_on D2, D3, DRIVER_DURABILITY:
  |
    Propose deterministic Cloud Tasks names from authoring outbox ID and
    generation. Mutation paths dispatch after commit, application startup scans
    pending rows, and stale dispatched rows re-arm only when no active matching
    lease exists. A 409 for the same deterministic name is successful idempotent
    enqueue; terminal work closes its outbox.

decision D5 based_on DRIVER_EXISTING_CONTRACT, D2, D3, D4:
  |
    Propose one authoring worker queue per deployed environment, shared by all
    users and all three authoring families. Initially reuse the configured Cloud
    Tasks transport project, location, physical queue, and service account while
    giving authoring its own target URL, audience, task namespace, outbox,
    endpoint, and fenced ownership. Sharing transport does not share narration
    persistence or entity mutation authority. A later dedicated physical queue
    may isolate throughput without changing the environment-global scheduling or
    owner-scoped execution contracts.

decision D6 based_on D1, D2, D3, D4, D5:
  |
    Acceptance authorizes local schema migration, repositories, dispatcher,
    endpoint, command-path wake, startup recovery, configuration, workflow
    checks, and regression tests. It does not authorize migration execution,
    cloud configuration, push, release, Stage or production deployment.

decision ACCEPTANCE based_on D1, D2, D3, D4, D5, D6, OWNER_ACCEPTANCE:
  |
    ADR-0024 is Accepted at this owner-approved revision. Local implementation
    of D1 through D6 is authorized within the stated external-action boundary.
